\begin_layout Title
Use of AI Assistance in This Work
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Bampaliaris Nikolaos
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
03-11-2026
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Summary
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An AI assistant, Claude (Anthropic), used through the Claude Code tool, was used during this thesis in three roles: to 
\series bold
implement
\series default
 the numerical code that evaluates the mathematical model, to 
\series bold
typeset
\series default
 text and equations in LyX, and as an 
\series bold
advisor
\series default
 for discussing problems and options. The physical model, its mathematical derivation, the modelling decisions and the interpretation of the results are the author's. Every output of the assistant was reviewed by the author, who directed the work and made the final decisions. This note records the division of work so that it can be stated accurately in the thesis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Work Done by the Author
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Mathematical model.
\series default
 Derivation of the field equations of the space charge and of its images in the rod and in the grounded plane (the vectors, the distances 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{1}$
\end_inset
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{5}$
\end_inset
 and the integrals), and of the drift kinematics, in the first version of the main document.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Figures.
\series default
 All vector schematics of the document, including their redrawing for the apex coordinate system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Data.
\series default
 Digitization of Figure 3 of the paper with WebPlotDigitizer, and all plots of the results in Excel.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Direction and decisions.
\series default
 Choice of what to reproduce and how, and approval of every modelling decision (for example measuring all distances from the rod apex, including the drift in Figure 5, the impulse tail, and treating the inception voltage as a measured input).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Critical review.
\series default
 Identification of problems in the results and the text, for example the paper's 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 exceeding the streamer-region field, the apparent shift of the digitized data, unclear or unsupported statements in drafts, and the choice of notation (e.g. 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu^{+}$
\end_inset
 for the ion mobility).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Understanding and verification.
\series default
 Review of all code results and of every argument and equation in the documents.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Work Done with the AI Assistant
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Numerical Code
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The assistant wrote the Python implementation of the author's equations and the supporting scripts, following the author's specifications:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the field functions and solvers in the solver/ package (space-charge and image fields as numerical integrals, the solution for 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
, the drift integration, the impulse fit) and their conversion to SI units and to the apex coordinate system;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the reproduction scripts for Figures 3, 4 and 5, which write their results to Excel;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the tools compare_datasets.py (comparison with digitized data) and export_excel_charts.py (export of the author's Excel charts as vector figures).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Writing this code without assistance would have required learning the numerical libraries in detail (SciPy integration, root finding and ODE solvers, openpyxl), which was not the subject of the thesis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Typesetting and Drafting in LyX
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Edits of the author's derivations:
\series default
 conversion of the equations of Sections 2 to 4 to the apex coordinate system and correction of small typographical errors, following the author's redrawn figures.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Drafts written from the analyses and the author's decisions:
\series default
 the term-by-term comparison with the paper (Section 3), applying the drift to the image fields (Section 4.2), the impulse voltage (Section 4.3), the summary of the framework (Section 5) and the reproduction chapter (Section 6), all revised at the author's request.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
The analysis notes
\series default
 01 to 05 in Theory/Notes/, written from the analyses carried out in the project.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Advice and Analysis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
explanation of background topics on request (e.g. corona inception, the meaning of Figures 6 to 8 of the paper), and recommendations among options, which the author accepted or rejected;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
checks proposed by the assistant or the author and carried out with the code: digitization error, sweeps and fits of the unreported parameters, the shift and peak analysis of Figure 3, and the test of the paper's misprinted formulas;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
numerical verification that the equations in the document agree with the code.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Review of AI Output
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The output of the assistant was not accepted without review. During the work the author found and had corrected, among others, a description of a comparison chart that did not match how the chart was made, explanations judged too long or based on numbers the reader could not trace, numerical tables that did not belong in a purely mathematical section, and inconsistent notation. Errors of the assistant found by itself during verification were also corrected. All numerical results in the thesis come from the scripts in the repository and can be reproduced.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Division of Work
\end_layout

